- What are memory implications for dense vs sparse alphabets?
- How would you support delete of non-existent words without errors?

## Concept Overview
An LRU (Least Recently Used) cache efficiently manages memory by evicting the least accessed items when capacity is reached. This implementation leverages a HashMap for fast key lookups and a DoublyLinkedList for maintaining access order.

## Implementation Details
- **HashMap**: Maps keys to Node references, enabling O(1) lookup operations
- **DoublyLinkedList**: Maintains access order with head representing most recently used items and tail representing least recently used items
- **Thread Safety**: ReentrantReadWriteLock provides optimal concurrency - multiple threads can read simultaneously while writes are mutually exclusive
- **Eviction Policy**: When capacity is exceeded, the tail node (least recently used) is removed
- **Operations**: Both get() and put() operations maintain O(1) time complexity

## Code Example
```java
public class LRUCache<K, V> {
    private final Map<K, Node<K, V>> cache;
    private final DoublyLinkedList<K, V> order;
    private final ReentrantReadWriteLock lock;
    private final int capacity;

    public LRUCache(int capacity) {
        this.capacity = capacity;
        this.cache = new HashMap<>();
        this.order = new DoublyLinkedList<>();
        this.lock = new ReentrantReadWriteLock();
    }

    public V get(K key) {
        lock.readLock().lock();
        try {
            Node<K, V> node = cache.get(key);
            if (node != null) {
                // Upgrade to write lock for reordering
                lock.readLock().unlock();
                lock.writeLock().lock();
                order.moveToHead(node);
                return node.value;
            }
            return null;
        } finally {
            if (lock.isWriteLocked()) {
                lock.writeLock().unlock();
            } else {
                lock.readLock().unlock();
            }
        }
    }

    public void put(K key, V value) {
        lock.writeLock().lock();
        try {
            Node<K, V> existing = cache.get(key);
            if (existing != null) {
                existing.value = value;
                order.moveToHead(existing);
                return;
            }

            Node<K, V> newNode = new Node<>(key, value);
            cache.put(key, newNode);
            order.addToHead(newNode);

            if (cache.size() > capacity) {
                Node<K, V> tail = order.removeTail();
                cache.remove(tail.key);
            }
        } finally {
            lock.writeLock().unlock();
        }
    }
}```

## Why This Is Asked
Tests ability to derive a recurrence for a simple constraint (no adjacent picks), then implement both top-down memoization and bottom-up tabulation. Requires correct base cases for N=0 and N=1 and translating to iterative state transitions. Follow-up prompts space optimization and practical coding habits.

## Key Concepts
- DP for non-adjacent max sum (House Robber)
- Top-down memoization with base cases
- Bottom-up tabulation
- Space optimization to O(1)

## Code Example
```javascript
function maxNonAdjacent(nums){
  const n = nums.length;
  if (n === 0) return 0;
  const memo = Array(n).fill(-1);
  const solve = i => {
    if (i < 0) return 0;
    if (memo[i] !== -1) return memo[i];
    memo[i] = Math.max(nums[i] + solve(i-2), solve(i-1));
    return memo[i];
  };
  return solve(n-1);
}
function maxNonAdjacentTab(nums){
  const n = nums.length;
  if (n === 0) return 0;
  let prev2 = 0, prev1 = Math.max(0, nums[0]);
  for (let i = 1; i < n; i++){
    const cur = Math.max(prev1, nums[i] + prev2);
    prev2 = prev1;
    prev1 = cur;
  }
  return prev1;
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you handle negative values or all-negative arrays?
- How would you adapt to a similar constraint with k-adjacent bans (no more than two chosen in any window of size k)?

## Why This Is Asked

This question tests adaptive sorting algorithms, merge logic, and the ability to reason about time complexity in practical, maintainable ways.

## Key Concepts

- **Adaptive sorting**: Using a pre-check to achieve O(n) best-case performance when the array is already sorted
- **Iterative bottom-up mergesort**: Avoiding recursion by iteratively merging sorted subarrays of increasing size
- **Stability and memory usage**: Understanding the trade-offs of O(n) auxiliary space for guaranteed stability

## Code Example

```javascript
function sortWithEarlyMerge(arr) {
  // Early return if already sorted
  for (let i = 1; i < arr.length; i++) {
    if (arr[i] < arr[i - 1]) break;
    if (i === arr.length - 1) return arr;
  }

  // Iterative bottom-up mergesort
  const aux = [...arr];
  let width = 1;

  while (width < arr.length) {
    for (let i = 0; i < arr.length; i += width * 2) {
      const left = i;
      const mid = Math.min(i + width, arr.length);
      const right = Math.min(i + width * 2, arr.length);

      let p = left, q = mid, k = left;
      while (p < mid && q < right) {
        aux[k++] = arr[p] <= arr[q] ? arr[p++] : arr[q++];
      }
      while (p < mid) aux[k++] = arr[p++];
      while (q < right) aux[k++] = arr[q++];
    }

    [arr, aux] = [aux, arr];
    width *= 2;
  }

  return arr;
}
```

## Time Complexity Analysis

- **Best case (O(n))**: Array is already sorted; single linear scan confirms this
- **Worst case (O(n log n))**: Array is reverse sorted; full mergesort required
- **Space complexity**: O(n) for the auxiliary buffer

## Why This Is Asked
Tests data-structure augmentation in a Trie to support prefix queries and k-th item retrieval with weights, mirroring autocomplete and ranking systems.

## Key Concepts
- Trie augmentation with per-subtree weights
- Prefix sum queries in O(length of prefix)
- K-th word selection within a prefix using cumulative weights
- Handling duplicates and deletions robustly

## Code Example
```javascript
class TrieNode { constructor(){ this.next=new Map(); this.subWeight=0; this.endWeight=0; this.isWord=false; } }
class Trie {
  constructor(){ this.root=new TrieNode(); }
  insert(word,w){ let node=this.root; node.subWeight+=w; for(let ch of word){ if(!node.next.has(ch)) node.next.set(ch,new TrieNode()); node=node.next.get(ch); node.subWeight+=w; } node.isWord=true; node.endWeight+=w; }
  delete(word,w){ this.insert(word,-w); }
  sumPrefix(prefix){ let node=this.root; for(let ch of prefix){ if(!node.next.has(ch)) return 0; node=node.next.get(ch); } return node.subWeight; }
  kthWord(prefix,k){ let node=this.root; for(let ch of prefix){ if(!node.next.has(ch)) return null; node=node.next.get(ch); }
    function dfs(n, path){ if(n.isWord && n.endWeight>0){ if(--k===0) return path; }
      for(let c of [...n.next.keys()].sort()){ let child=n.next.get(c); if(child.subWeight>=k){ const res=dfs(child, path+c); if(res) return res; } else { k-=child.subWeight; } }
      return null; }
    return dfs(node, prefix);
  }
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you handle Unicode alphabets or memory limits?
- How would you support tie-breaking for equal weights?
- How would you optimize for bulk updates and high deletion rates?

## Why This Is Asked
Demonstrates combined data structures and time-based eviction. Tests ability to design per-key streaming aggregates with real-time top-K, using arrays/linked lists/hashtable/heap and lazy deletions.

## Key Concepts
- Sliding window aggregation; - Hash maps for per-tag buckets; - Deques for per-tag event queues; - Max-heap with lazy invalidation for top-K.

## Code Example
```javascript
class SlidingTagTopK {
  constructor(windowMs){ this.W=windowMs; this.tagMap=new Map(); this.heap=[]; this.ver=0; }
  _expire(tag, now){ const b=this.tagMap.get(tag); while (b.queue.length && b.queue[0].ts <= now - this.W){ b.sum -= b.queue.shift().val; } }
  addEvent(tag, userId, value, ts){ let b=this.tagMap.get(tag); if(!b){ b={sum:0, queue:[], ver:0}; this.tagMap.set(tag,b);} this._expire(tag, ts); b.queue.push({ts, val: value}); b.sum += value; this.ver++; // push new entry
    this._heapPush({sum: b.sum, tag, ver:this.ver}); }
  topK(K){ // lazy
    const res=[]; const seen=new Set(); while (res.length<K && this.heap.length){ const top=this._heapPop(); if (top.ver===this.tagMap.get(top.tag).ver){ res.push(top.tag); seen.add(top.tag); } }
    return res;
  }
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you handle deletions or varying window sizes per tag?
- How would you optimize memory and support concurrent producers?

